[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) holds every report about dates since April, merged on subject line
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). Read by what the reports show, not by their
subject, it is three problems with three fixes. Each now has its own ticket.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: day and month swapped.** The shared parser reads every date month first, and every
tenant on file writes the day first. "4 March turned into 3 April." This is the bug #4471 names,
and the fix queued for it covers only this row.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form.** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People type
what it says, and the form reads their date day first. Same symptom, a swap, but it only comes
through the form at the three booking-v3 tenants, and the parser fix does not touch it.

**`one-day move`: off by one day.** These are no swap at all. A date moves by exactly one day, or
two people in different time zones see different days for the same meeting. That points to time zone
handling, not to date order. The desk had already listed them in `desk-notes.json`; the cause
table filed them under `parser` by tenant, so nobody counted them.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not "one issue, one fix queued". Shipping the parser fix leaves the other two rows open.
</Callout>

## Why they were merged

- Decision 0001 merges on subject alone. All three problems produce the same subjects ("Wrong day
  on the invite"), so subject matching cannot tell them apart.
- Intake tags (`meta.intakeTag`) come from the reporter's browser and disagree with the cause in
  both directions. The cause comes from the tenant: their locale, their booking form, and the
  channel the report came through.

## What to do next time

- Before merging into a big ticket, check the cause with the [triage explorer](/kb/triage/dedupe),
  not the subject.
- A report that is no swap (one day off, differs by place) is a different problem. Give it a ticket.

